Selector identity: documentation anchors, not JSON pointers
-----------------------------------------------------------

This connector's dialect is `postgresql-docs-16`. Its source references are
documentation anchors of the form `#<anchor>` (`#SQL-SELECT`,
`#INFOSCHEMA-COLUMNS`), not RFC 6901 JSON pointers: the pinned documents are
HTML, so a pointer into them would be a fabricated accessor. The compiler's
documentation grammar admits an anchor as hyphen-separated ASCII
letter/digit segments that begin with a letter, and refuses every other shape;
positional documentation identifiers such as `id-1.9.3.172.1` are outside the
admitted grammar. Each declared anchor is verified against the pinned bytes: the
capture must contain exactly one start tag that declares `id="<anchor>"`, and
the source fact bound to that anchor is the SHA-256 of that exact start tag.
A malformed selector, a missing anchor, a duplicated anchor or markup whose
start tag cannot be resolved exactly refuses generation instead of lowering a
partial source.

Declared operations and their typed statement facts
---------------------------------------------------

Three operations are declared over one logical relation binding,
`postgres.reusable-relation`. The binding is a logical identity: a connection
binds it to one concrete schema/table (and its declared columns, key and
mappings) before any statement is built. No customer schema, table, column or
credential appears in this connector root.

  operation                 statement_kind  source anchors
  relation/metadata         metadata        #PROTOCOL, #INFORMATION-SCHEMA, #INFOSCHEMA-COLUMNS
  relation/select           select          #PROTOCOL, #PROTOCOL-FLOW-EXT-QUERY, #SQL-SELECT, #SQL-WHERE, #SQL-LIMIT, #SQL-ORDERBY
  relation/mapped-upsert    mapped_upsert   #PROTOCOL, #PROTOCOL-FLOW-EXT-QUERY, #SQL-INSERT, #SQL-ON-CONFLICT, #SQL-UPDATE

Every operation declares an explicitly empty `request_variants`/`response_variants`
inventory (HTTP-shaped facts do not exist here), one `success` JSON typed-row
response derived by the compiler from the transport contract, the accepted
capability closure (`effect.authorize`, `effect.limit`, `sql.execute`,
`value.serialize`) and exactly the seven lane dispositions authored in
`semantics.json`. `semantics.json`'s `revision` is the SHA-256 of the canonical
JSON of its `operations` array, so it changes exactly when a declared semantic
amendment changes and can be re-derived by any consumer.

Capture status and verification
-------------------------------

Every captured page is stored complete; no bounded derivation is applied, so
the compiler input and the upstream page are the same bytes:

  python3 - <<'PY'
  import hashlib, json, pathlib
  lock = json.load(open("connectors/postgres/source.lock.json"))
  for document in lock["source_set"]["documents"]:
      data = pathlib.Path("connectors/postgres/captures", document["source_id"]).read_bytes()
      assert len(data) == document["byte_length"], document["source_id"]
      assert hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() == document["sha256"], document["source_id"]
  PY

A consumer that lowers this source MUST re-verify every captured page against
the byte length and SHA-256 in `source.lock.json` and the retained licence
against its own declared hash; a mismatch is a blocker, never a substitution.
Re-capturing a page from `postgresql.org` may legitimately produce different
bytes (the documentation site is regenerated per release); such a capture
requires a new explicit source identity, not an edited hash.

Source-facing, not provider-facing
----------------------------------

Captured documentation declarations and generated projections are build-time and
runtime facts about the documented interface. They are not execution evidence,
not account-scope evidence, not live provider certification and not proof that a
customer relation, key, constraint or writable-column mapping exists. The
lifecycle scenarios under `scenarios/lifecycle.json` declare intended observations
for the authorized fixture connections named in their requirements; a controlled
PostgreSQL-compatible fixture remains fake evidence, and live account evidence
requires separate authorization and its own receipts.
